WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.510
The software stack begins with an operating-system image.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.810
Provider images may include GPU drivers and acceleration libraries,

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.635
while general images require installation.

00:00:08.635 --> 00:00:13.084
Match the NVIDIA driver, CUDA runtime, framework, and GPU architecture deliberately.

00:00:13.084 --> 00:00:15.820
A command that detects the GPU is only the first test;

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.440
confirm that the chosen framework can allocate memory and execute a representative

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.440
operation.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.710
Containers package applications and dependencies into repeatable units,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.340
but they still rely on a compatible host driver and GPU container runtime.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.730
Pin image versions or immutable digests instead of pulling an uncontrolled latest tag.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.320
For non-container workflows,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.820
create isolated Python environments and record package versions.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.430
Notebooks are useful for exploration,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:42.540
but a long-lived notebook with manual changes is difficult to reproduce and easy to expose

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.420
accidentally.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.780
Model servers turn the instance into a service,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.385
and local API endpoints let applications communicate with them.

00:00:49.385 --> 00:00:53.120
Bind development services to a private interface or loopback address until access controls

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.096
are ready.

00:00:54.096 --> 00:00:57.960
Document model versions, startup arguments, ports, context settings, and health checks.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.125
If a web notebook or model interface must be reached remotely,

00:01:01.125 --> 00:01:04.930
prefer an authenticated private access path over opening a management port to the

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:05.665
internet.

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:09.280
Automate setup with reviewed scripts, images, or infrastructure definitions.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.020
The goal is to replace a failed or temporary instance without reconstructing it from

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:13.600
memory.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.360
Keep secrets and private data outside reusable images.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.140
Test the build on a fresh instance, measure provisioning time,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.501
and preserve only the components necessary for the workload.

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:25.990
Reproducibility reduces both operational risk and the billable time spent repairing

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.000
one-off environments.
